ng> Solve the system:  x + y = 17  and  x - y = -3</p><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 10, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 7, y = 10</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 8, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 17, y = -3</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 14  →  x = 7;  then y = 17 - 7 = 10</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  4x = 36</p><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 32</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 40</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide both sides by 4: x = 36 / 4 = 9</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 11  and  x - y = 9</p><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 1, y = 10</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 11, y = 0</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 11, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 10, y = 1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 20  →  x = 10;  then y = 11 - 10 = 1</div></div></div>','grade10_math.pdf, page 63','Approved','2026-05-24 17:21:21','grade10_math.pdf',63,10,0,0),(45,1,'Practice: Relations and Functions 1 RELATIONS AND FUNCTIONS Unit Outcomes','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 1: Relations and Functions 1 RELATIONS AND FUNCTIONS Unit Outcomes \nDefine relation. Sketch graphs of various types of relations and functions.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>a. 𝑓( ) = 4 + 1;     = −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3</code></li><li><code>b. 𝑓( ) = −2 + 5;    = −1, −0.5, 0, 2, 3, 4</code></li><li><code>a. 𝑓( ) =  + 2: 𝑓(0) = 2,</code></li><li><code>b. 𝑔( ) = − + 2 : 𝑔(0) = 2,</code></li><li><code>c. ℎ( ) = 2 + 2 : ℎ(0) = 2,</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>start with an example taken from everyday life in the relationship of fatherhood. The problem we set for ourselves is to describe this relation in mathematical terms. If you think about this problem for a few minutes, you will probably find it somewhat more difficult than you expected. Although everyone knows what it means to say that is a father of  , it is not quite so clear how to put this in the language we use in mathematics. In our daily life, we usually talk about relations between variou</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> A bag contains 12 identical objects, of which 7 are boy. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a boy?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 5/12</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 7/5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7/12</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 12/7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(boy) = favorable / total = 7/12</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> A bag contains 8 identical objects, of which 1 are king. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a king?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 7/8</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/7</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8/1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — P(king) = favorable / total = 1/8</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> A bag contains 10 identical objects, of which 7 are black ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a black ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 7/10</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 3/10</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7/3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 10/7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — P(black ball) = favorable / total = 7/10</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> A bag contains 6 identical objects, of which 4 are tails. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a tails?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 4/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 4/6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6/4</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(tails) = favorable / total = 4/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q5.</strong> A fair 6-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 5?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 5/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1/5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 1/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — There is 1 favorable outcome (rolling a 5) out of 6 total. P(5) = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q6.</strong> A bag contains 7 identical objects, of which 1 are heads. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a heads?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 6/7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7/1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 1/7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — P(heads) = favorable / total = 1/7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q7.</strong> A bag contains 9 identical objects, of which 8 are white ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a white ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/9</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 8/9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 8/1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 9/8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — P(white ball) = favorable / total = 8/9</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 10 Mathematics Textbook, page 10 — Unit 1: Relations and Functions 1 RELATIONS AND FUNCTIONS Unit Outcomes','Approved','2026-05-24 20:22:33','grade10_math.pdf',10,NULL,0,0),(46,1,'Practice: Algebra','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 8x + 12 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 3 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 2 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -2 and x = -6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 12 and x = 8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Factor: (x - 2)(x - 6) = 0  →  x = 2 or x = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 14x + 49 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 7 (double root)</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 8 and x = 8</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -7 and x = -7</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 49 and x = 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 7)(x - 7) = 0  →  x = 7 or x = 7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 8x + 7 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 1 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 2 and x = 8</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -1 and x = -7</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 7 and x = 8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 1)(x - 7) = 0  →  x = 1 or x = 7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 10x + 25 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 6 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 5 (double root)</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -5 and x = -5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 25 and x = 10</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Factor: (x - 5)(x - 5) = 0  →  x = 5 or x = 5</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61 — Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions','Approved','2026-05-24 22:12:40','grade9_math.pdf',61,NULL,0,0),(47,7,'Practice: Quadratic Equation','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>A system of linear equation in two variables can be solved by one of the \nfollowing methods: graphically, or by substitution or by elimination. The absolute value of a number 𝑥, denoted by |𝑥|, is defined as the distance \n|𝑥| from zero on a number line. For any non- negative number 𝑝, |𝑥| = 𝑝  means 𝑥= 𝑝  or 𝑥= −𝑝.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>9. For any non- negative number 𝑝, |𝑥| = 𝑝  means 𝑥= 𝑝  or 𝑥= −𝑝.</code></li><li><code>10. An equation of the form 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0  where a, b, c, ∈ R and 𝑎≠0  is a</code></li><li><code>can be used to solve a quadratic equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0</code></li><li><code>12. If the quadratic equation, 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, has roots</code></li><li><code>13. The discriminant of quadratic equations, 𝐷= 𝑏2 −4𝑎𝑐,</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>d. 𝑥2 −4𝑥+ 3 = 0 Example 1 Example 2 Exercise 3.10 128</li><li>Example 2  Write down the standard form of the equation of a circle with the given centre and radius. a C (0, 0), r = 8 b C (2, –7), r = 9 Solution a h = k = 0 and r = 8 Therefore, the equation of the circle is (x − 0)2 + (y − 0)2 = 82.</li><li>Example 3  Write the standard form of the equation of the circle with centre at C (2, 3) and that passes through the point P (7, –3). x y P(x, y) r C(h, k) •</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 17  and  x - y = 1</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 8, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 10, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 9, y = 8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 17, y = 1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 18  →  x = 9;  then y = 17 - 9 = 8</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  9x = 72</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 81</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Divide both sides by 9: x = 72 / 9 = 8</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 8 = 30</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 22</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 24</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 25</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 20</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Subtract 8 from both sides: x = 30 - 8 = 22</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 4 = 32</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 28</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 29</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 30</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 31</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Subtract 4 from both sides: x = 32 - 4 = 28</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159 — Unit 3: Solving Equations Factorization Method 2 Solve the quadratic equation 𝑥2 + 6𝑥+ 9 = 0','Approved','2026-05-24 22:12:41','grade9_math.pdf',159,NULL,0,0),(48,7,'Practice: quadratic','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 3: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method \nThere are quadratic equations that cannot be solved by factorization method. This is \ngenerally true when the roots are not rational numbers. Consider the quadratic \nequation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, where 𝑎≠0.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, where 𝑎≠0.</code></li><li><code>2𝑎 Solve 𝑥2 −6𝑥+ 3 = 0 using the quadratic formula.</code></li><li><code>For the quadratic equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, 𝑎≠0, 𝒙=</code></li><li><code>Unit 3: Solving Equations Factorization Method 2 Solve the quadratic equation 𝑥2 + 6𝑥+ 9 = 0</code></li><li><code>ሺ𝑥+ 3ሻሺ𝑥+ 3ሻ= ሺ𝑥+ 3ሻ2 = 0 (Factorizing: sum = 6  and product = 9).</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>d. 𝑥2 −4𝑥+ 3 = 0 Example 1 Example 2 Exercise 3.10 128</li><li>The rest of the solution is the same as Example 3 above. Therefore, the solution of this quadratic inequality is  𝑥≤−3  or  𝑥≥4. Alternatively, the solution is (−∞, −3] ∪[4,   ∞). Exercise 4.8 Example 3 Example 4 173</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 11  and  x - y = 5</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 3, y = 8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 9, y = 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 11, y = 5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 8, y = 3</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 16  →  x = 8;  then y = 11 - 8 = 3</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  10x = 60</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 70</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Divide both sides by 10: x = 60 / 10 = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  11x = 143</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 132</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 13</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 12</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide both sides by 11: x = 143 / 11 = 13</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 12x + 35 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 7 and x = 5</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 8 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -7 and x = -5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 35 and x = 12</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 7)(x - 5) = 0  →  x = 7 or x = 5</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143 — Unit 3: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method','Approved','2026-05-24 22:14:58','grade9_math.pdf',143,NULL,0,0),(49,1,'Practice: Trigonometric Function 199 TRIGONOMETRIC FUNCTIONS Unit Outcomes','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 5: Introduction to Trigonometry (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) Trigonometric Ratios of 𝟎° to  𝟗𝟎° \nWe discussed how to determine trigonometric ratio of 30°, 45° and 60° using \nexamples. To derive the trigonometric ratios of  0° and 90° needs advanced concept \nof mathematics and will not be treated at this level. The trigonometric ratios of these \ntwo angles together with 30°, 45° and 60°  are indicated in the following table 5.1.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>sine of ∠𝐴=</code></li><li><code>cosine of ∠𝐴=</code></li><li><code>tangent of ∠𝐴=</code></li><li><code>side adjacent to angle 𝐴=</code></li><li><code>a. Using definition 5.2, sin 𝐴=</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Trigonometric Identities Here, we will prove the above trigonometric identities. Take an example of a right- angled triangle 𝛥ABC as shown in figure 4.33. Proof of Trigonometric Identities In a right-angled triangle, by the Pythagoras Theorem, we know (Perpendicular side length) 2 +  (Base length)2 = (Hypotenuse length)2 Therefore, in 𝛥ABC, we have; (AB)2 + (BC)2 = (AC)2                                . . . (1)</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Convert 30° to radians (in terms of π)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. π/3</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. π/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. π/6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 0</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — 30° × (π/180) = 30π/180 = π/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Find: cos(30°)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/2</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. √3/2</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 0</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Using the unit circle / special angles table: cos(30°) = √3/2</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find: cos(90°)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 90/180</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 0</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Using the unit circle / special angles table: cos(90°) = 0</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find: cos(0°)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 0/180</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Using the unit circle / special angles table: cos(0°) = 1</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q5.</strong> Convert 90° to radians (in terms of π)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. π</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. π/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. π/4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 0</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — 90° × (π/180) = 90π/180 = π/2</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q6.</strong> Convert 45° to radians (in terms of π)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. π/4</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. π/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. π</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 0</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — 45° × (π/180) = 45π/180 = π/4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q7.</strong> Find: sin(90°)</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 90/180</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Using the unit circle / special angles table: sin(90°) = 1</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 208 — Unit 5: Introduction to Trigonometry (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) Trigonometric Ratios of 𝟎° to  𝟗𝟎°','Approved','2026-05-24 22:18:12','grade9_math.pdf',208,NULL,0,0),(50,1,'Practice: algebra','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve for x:  6x = 36</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 5</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 42</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Divide both sides by 6: x = 36 / 6 = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  7x = 42</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 35</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide both sides by 7: x = 42 / 7 = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 14  and  x - y = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 7, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 7, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 8, y = 6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 14, y = 0</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 14  →  x = 7;  then y = 14 - 7 = 7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 1 = 29</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 26</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 28</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 27</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 29</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Subtract 1 from both sides: x = 29 - 1 = 28</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61 — Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions','Approved','2026-05-24 22:29:40','grade9_math.pdf',61,NULL,0,0),(51,1,'Practice: algebra','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve for x:  11x = 77</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 66</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Divide both sides by 11: x = 77 / 11 = 7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  6x = 66</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 72</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 10</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 12</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 11</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Divide both sides by 6: x = 66 / 6 = 11</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  3x = 45</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 42</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 14</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 15</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 48</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Divide both sides by 3: x = 45 / 3 = 15</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  2x = 22</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 10</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 12</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 13</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 11</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Divide both sides by 2: x = 22 / 2 = 11</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61 — Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions','Approved','2026-05-24 22:30:53','grade9_math.pdf',61,NULL,0,0),(52,1,'Practice: algebra','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 11 = 31</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 17</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 19</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 20</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 23</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Subtract 11 from both sides: x = 31 - 11 = 20</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 18 = 38</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 17</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 21</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 22</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 20</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Subtract 18 from both sides: x = 38 - 18 = 20</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 9x + 8 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 8 and x = 1</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 9 and x = 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -8 and x = -1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 8 and x = 9</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 8)(x - 1) = 0  →  x = 8 or x = 1</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 6x + 8 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 5 and x = 3</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = -4 and x = -2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 8 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 4 and x = 2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Factor: (x - 4)(x - 2) = 0  →  x = 4 or x = 2</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61 — Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions','Approved','2026-05-24 22:46:21','grade9_math.pdf',61,NULL,0,0),(53,1,'Practice: algebra','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 11x + 30 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 6 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = -5 and x = -6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 30 and x = 11</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 5 and x = 6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Factor: (x - 5)(x - 6) = 0  →  x = 5 or x = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 8  and  x - y = -4</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 2, y = 6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 6, y = 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 3, y = 5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 8, y = -4</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 4  →  x = 2;  then y = 8 - 2 = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 11 = 15</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 5</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Subtract 11 from both sides: x = 15 - 11 = 4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 4  and  x - y = -2</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 1, y = 3</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 3, y = 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 2, y = 2</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 4, y = -2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 2  →  x = 1;  then y = 4 - 1 = 3</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61 — Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions','Approved','2026-05-24 22:46:43','grade9_math.pdf',61,NULL,0,0),(54,1,'Practice: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 3: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method \nThere are quadratic equations that cannot be solved by factorization method. This is \ngenerally true when the roots are not rational numbers. Consider the quadratic \nequation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, where 𝑎≠0.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, where 𝑎≠0.</code></li><li><code>2𝑎 Solve 𝑥2 −6𝑥+ 3 = 0 using the quadratic formula.</code></li><li><code>For the quadratic equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, 𝑎≠0, 𝒙=</code></li><li><code>1. ሺ𝑥+ 3ሻሺ𝑥−2ሻ= 0</code></li><li><code>2. ሺ5𝑥+ 1ሻሺ2𝑥+ 4ሻ= 2</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>d. 𝑥2 −4𝑥+ 3 = 0 Example 1 Example 2 Exercise 3.10 128</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 19  and  x - y = 1</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 9, y = 10</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 10, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 11, y = 8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 19, y = 1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 20  →  x = 10;  then y = 19 - 10 = 9</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 11x + 24 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 9 and x = 4</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = -8 and x = -3</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 8 and x = 3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 24 and x = 11</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Factor: (x - 8)(x - 3) = 0  →  x = 8 or x = 3</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 14x + 48 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 8 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 9 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -8 and x = -6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 48 and x = 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 8)(x - 6) = 0  →  x = 8 or x = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 3 = 18</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 15</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 16</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 17</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 13</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Subtract 3 from both sides: x = 18 - 3 = 15</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q5.</strong> Solve for x:  5x = 25</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 4</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 10</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 5</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Divide both sides by 5: x = 25 / 5 = 5</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q6.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 11  and  x - y = 1</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 5, y = 6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 6, y = 5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 7, y = 4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 11, y = 1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 12  →  x = 6;  then y = 11 - 6 = 5</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q7.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 16 = 44</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 25</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 26</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 27</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 28</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Subtract 16 from both sides: x = 44 - 16 = 28</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143 — Unit 3: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method','Approved','2026-05-24 23:00:54','grade9_math.pdf',143,NULL,0,0),(55,7,'Practice: quadratic equation','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>A system of linear equation in two variables can be solved by one of the \nfollowing methods: graphically, or by substitution or by elimination. The absolute value of a number 𝑥, denoted by |𝑥|, is defined as the distance \n|𝑥| from zero on a number line. For any non- negative number 𝑝, |𝑥| = 𝑝  means 𝑥= 𝑝  or 𝑥= −𝑝.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>9. For any non- negative number 𝑝, |𝑥| = 𝑝  means 𝑥= 𝑝  or 𝑥= −𝑝.</code></li><li><code>10. An equation of the form 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0  where a, b, c, ∈ R and 𝑎≠0  is a</code></li><li><code>can be used to solve a quadratic equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0</code></li><li><code>12. If the quadratic equation, 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, has roots</code></li><li><code>13. The discriminant of quadratic equations, 𝐷= 𝑏2 −4𝑎𝑐,</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>d. 𝑥2 −4𝑥+ 3 = 0 Example 1 Example 2 Exercise 3.10 128</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 16  and  x - y = 2</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 7, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 9, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 10, y = 6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 16, y = 2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 18  →  x = 9;  then y = 16 - 9 = 7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 11  and  x - y = 7</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 2, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 9, y = 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 10, y = 1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 11, y = 7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 18  →  x = 9;  then y = 11 - 9 = 2</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 13 = 14</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 4</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Subtract 13 from both sides: x = 14 - 13 = 1</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 14x + 48 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 7 and x = 9</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 6 and x = 8</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -6 and x = -8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 48 and x = 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Factor: (x - 6)(x - 8) = 0  →  x = 6 or x = 8</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159 — Unit 3: Solving Equations Factorization Method 2 Solve the quadratic equation 𝑥2 + 6𝑥+ 9 = 0','Approved','2026-05-25 09:15:15','grade9_math.pdf',159,NULL,0,0),(56,1,'Practice: Algebra','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 9x + 20 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 4 and x = 5</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 5 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -4 and x = -5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 20 and x = 9</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 4)(x - 5) = 0  →  x = 4 or x = 5</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  10x = 90</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 100</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 10</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide both sides by 10: x = 90 / 10 = 9</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  7x = 105</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 98</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 14</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 15</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 112</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Divide both sides by 7: x = 105 / 7 = 15</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 13x + 42 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 8 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = -7 and x = -6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 7 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 42 and x = 13</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Factor: (x - 7)(x - 6) = 0  →  x = 7 or x = 6</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 61 — Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions','Approved','2026-05-25 10:22:44','grade9_math.pdf',61,NULL,0,0),(57,5,'Practice: probablity','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: The Number System 2.2.2  Conversion of repeating decimals into fractions \nIn section 2.2.1, you have discussed how to convert the terminating decimals into \nfractions. In this subsection, you will learn how repeating decimals can be converted \nto fractions. Represent each of the following decimals as a simplest fraction form (ratio of two \nintegers).</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>𝑏 form.  Now let 𝑑= 0. 5̅. Then,</code></li><li><code>10𝑑= 5. 5̅       (multiplying both sides of  𝑑= 0. 5̅ by 10)</code></li><li><code>−    𝑑= 0. 5̅        (we use subtraction to eliminate the repeating part)</code></li><li><code>Now dividing both sides by 9, we have  𝑑=</code></li><li><code>𝑏 form. Now let  𝑑= 2. 12</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c.  𝑓(𝑥) = 𝑥3 −3𝑥2 −6 and 𝑔(𝑥) = −𝑥+ 1. Example 5 Exercise 2.8</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> A fair 6-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 4?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 4/6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 2/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — There is 1 favorable outcome (rolling a 4) out of 6 total. P(4) = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> A bag contains 6 identical objects, of which 1 are green ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a green ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 5/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1/5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6/1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(green ball) = favorable / total = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> A bag contains 8 identical objects, of which 6 are white ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a white ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2/8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 6/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 6/8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(white ball) = favorable / total = 6/8</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> A bag contains 12 identical objects, of which 4 are blue ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a blue ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 8/12</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 4/12</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 4/8</li><li 