">D. 12</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide both sides by 9: x = 99 / 9 = 11</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 8 = 37</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 26</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 27</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 29</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 31</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Subtract 8 from both sides: x = 37 - 8 = 29</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 9x + 14 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 3 and x = 8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 2 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -2 and x = -7</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 14 and x = 9</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Factor: (x - 2)(x - 7) = 0  →  x = 2 or x = 7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 9x + 18 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 6 and x = 3</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 7 and x = 4</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -6 and x = -3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 18 and x = 9</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Factor: (x - 6)(x - 3) = 0  →  x = 6 or x = 3</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 190','Approved','2026-05-25 17:56:57','grade12_math.pdf',190,11,0,0),(94,5,'Practice: probablity','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: Polynomial Functions 79 \nc. Is the degree of 𝑓∙𝑔 equal to the sum of the degrees of 𝑓 and 𝑔? 𝑓(𝑥) ∙𝑔(𝑥)  = (2𝑥+ 3)( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6) \n                 = 2𝑥( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6) + 3( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6)  (Distributive property) \n                 = ( 2𝑥)(3𝑥2) + (2𝑥)(−5𝑥) + (2𝑥)(6) + (3)(3𝑥2) + (3)(−5𝑥) + (3)(6)          \n                       (Distributive property)                                         \n                  = 6𝑥3 −10𝑥2 + 12𝑥+ 9𝑥2 −15𝑥+ 18 \n                  = 6𝑥3 + (−10𝑥2 + 9𝑥2) + (12𝑥−15𝑥) + 18 \n                  = 6𝑥3 −𝑥2 −3𝑥+ 18 \n    2.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>1.    𝑓(𝑥) ∙𝑔(𝑥)  = (2𝑥+ 3)( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6)</code></li><li><code>= 2𝑥( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6) + 3( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6)  (Distributive property)</code></li><li><code>= ( 2𝑥)(3𝑥2) + (2𝑥)(−5𝑥) + (2𝑥)(6) + (3)(3𝑥2) + (3)(−5𝑥) + (3)(6)</code></li><li><code>= 6𝑥3 −10𝑥2 + 12𝑥+ 9𝑥2 −15𝑥+ 18</code></li><li><code>= 6𝑥3 + (−10𝑥2 + 9𝑥2) + (12𝑥−15𝑥) + 18</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c. 180, 270, 1,080 Example 2 Exercise 2.6 41</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> A fair 6-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 1?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1/1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 5/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — There is 1 favorable outcome (rolling a 1) out of 6 total. P(1) = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> A bag contains 4 identical objects, of which 2 are queen. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a queen?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2/4</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 2/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 2/4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 4/2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(queen) = favorable / total = 2/4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> A bag contains 12 identical objects, of which 3 are yellow ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a yellow ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 9/12</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 3/12</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 3/9</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 12/3</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — P(yellow ball) = favorable / total = 3/12</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> A bag contains 6 identical objects, of which 1 are black ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a black ball?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 5/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6/1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — P(black ball) = favorable / total = 1/6</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 10 Mathematics Textbook, page 88 — Unit 2: Polynomial Functions 79','Approved','2026-05-25 17:56:57','grade10_math.pdf',88,10,0,0),(95,5,'Practice: probablity','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 1 Sequences and Series \n7 Note: \n \nA sequence that has a last term is called a finite sequence. A sequence that does \nnot have a last term is called an infinite sequence. The domain of a finite \nsequence is {1,2,3,...,\n}\n n .</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>a = and</code></li><li><code>= Thus, the first six terms of the sequence { }</code></li><li><code>Find the inverse of each relation and determine whether the inverse is a function. a    R ={(2, −2), (−3, 3), (−4, −4)}</code></li><li><code>Find the inverse of each function.  Is the inverse a function? a    f (x) = 2x + 3 b</code></li><li><code>compared with y = x3? i</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Example 4 a    The sequence, } { n p , where n</li><li>Example 5 Find the first six terms of the sequence{ } na defined recursively by 1 2 a = and 1 n</li><li>Example 6 The Fibonacci sequence nf is defined recursively by the conditions 1 2 n n 1 n 2 1,</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> A fair 6-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 3?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 44</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 3/6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 3/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — There is 1 favorable outcome (rolling a 3) out of 6 total. P(3) = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> A bag contains 12 identical objects, of which 3 are yellow ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a yellow ball?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 44</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 3/12</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 9/12</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 3/9</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 12/3</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — P(yellow ball) = favorable / total = 3/12</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> A bag contains 6 identical objects, of which 1 are girl. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a girl?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 44</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 5/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1/5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 6/1</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 1/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — P(girl) = favorable / total = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> A bag contains 8 identical objects, of which 7 are black ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a black ball?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 44</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 7/1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7/8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8/7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(black ball) = favorable / total = 7/8</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 15','Approved','2026-05-25 17:56:57','grade12_math.pdf',15,12,0,0),(96,1,'Practice: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 3: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method \nThere are quadratic equations that cannot be solved by factorization method. This is \ngenerally true when the roots are not rational numbers. Consider the quadratic \nequation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, where 𝑎≠0.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, where 𝑎≠0.</code></li><li><code>2𝑎 Solve 𝑥2 −6𝑥+ 3 = 0 using the quadratic formula.</code></li><li><code>For the quadratic equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, 𝑎≠0, 𝒙=</code></li><li><code>= 𝑎 2 + 𝑏 + 𝑐 = 𝑎 ( +</code></li><li><code>4𝑎2 = 𝑎 ( +</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>function is not sufficient. For example, the motion of an object thrown either upward or downward is modeled by a quadratic function. The graph of a quadratic function is a curve called a parabola. Parabolas may open upward or downward and vary in” width” or” steepness”, but they all have the same basic” U” shape. All parabolas are symmetric with respect to a line called the axis of symmetry. A parabola intersects its axis of symmetry at a point called the vertex of the parabola. Many quadratic </li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 5  and  x - y = -1</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 3, y = 2</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 3, y = 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 2, y = 3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 5, y = -1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 4  →  x = 2;  then y = 5 - 2 = 3</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 4x + 3 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 2 and x = 4</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 1 and x = 3</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = -1 and x = -3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 3 and x = 4</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Factor: (x - 1)(x - 3) = 0  →  x = 1 or x = 3</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  6x = 6</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 12</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Divide both sides by 6: x = 6 / 6 = 1</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 4 = 31</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 25</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 27</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 28</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 29</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Subtract 4 from both sides: x = 31 - 4 = 27</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q5.</strong> Find the roots of:  x² - 14x + 48 = 0</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 9 and x = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = -8 and x = -6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 8 and x = 6</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 48 and x = 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Factor: (x - 8)(x - 6) = 0  →  x = 8 or x = 6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q6.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 18  and  x - y = -2</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 10 Mathematics Textbook, page 63</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 10, y = 8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 9, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 18, y = -2</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 8, y = 10</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 16  →  x = 8;  then y = 18 - 8 = 10</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q7.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 15  and  x - y = -1</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 10 Mathematics Textbook, page 63</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 8, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 8, y = 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 7, y = 8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 15, y = -1</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 14  →  x = 7;  then y = 15 - 7 = 8</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 143 — Unit 3: Solving Equations The quadratic formula method','Approved','2026-05-25 17:58:03','grade9_math.pdf',143,9,0,0),(97,3,'Practice: integration','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 2: Polynomial Functions 79 \nc. Is the degree of 𝑓∙𝑔 equal to the sum of the degrees of 𝑓 and 𝑔? 𝑓(𝑥) ∙𝑔(𝑥)  = (2𝑥+ 3)( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6) \n                 = 2𝑥( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6) + 3( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6)  (Distributive property) \n                 = ( 2𝑥)(3𝑥2) + (2𝑥)(−5𝑥) + (2𝑥)(6) + (3)(3𝑥2) + (3)(−5𝑥) + (3)(6)          \n                       (Distributive property)                                         \n                  = 6𝑥3 −10𝑥2 + 12𝑥+ 9𝑥2 −15𝑥+ 18 \n                  = 6𝑥3 + (−10𝑥2 + 9𝑥2) + (12𝑥−15𝑥) + 18 \n                  = 6𝑥3 −𝑥2 −3𝑥+ 18 \n    2.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>1.    𝑓(𝑥) ∙𝑔(𝑥)  = (2𝑥+ 3)( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6)</code></li><li><code>= 2𝑥( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6) + 3( 3𝑥2 −5𝑥+ 6)  (Distributive property)</code></li><li><code>= ( 2𝑥)(3𝑥2) + (2𝑥)(−5𝑥) + (2𝑥)(6) + (3)(3𝑥2) + (3)(−5𝑥) + (3)(6)</code></li><li><code>= 6𝑥3 −10𝑥2 + 12𝑥+ 9𝑥2 −15𝑥+ 18</code></li><li><code>= 6𝑥3 + (−10𝑥2 + 9𝑥2) + (12𝑥−15𝑥) + 18</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>c. 180, 270, 1,080 Example 2 Exercise 2.6 41</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Find:  ∫ (6x + 4) dx</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. (6)x² + 4x + C</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. (3)x² + 5x + C</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 6 + C</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. (3)x² + 4x + C</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Integrate term by term: 6x²/2 + 4x + C = (3)x² + 4x + C</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Find:  ∫ (6x + 3) dx</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. (6)x² + 3x + C</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. (3)x² + 4x + C</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. (3)x² + 3x + C</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6 + C</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Integrate term by term: 6x²/2 + 3x + C = (3)x² + 3x + C</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find:  ∫ 10 dx</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 10x²/2 + C</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 11x + C</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 10x + C</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 9x + C</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — ∫ constant dx = constant·x + C  →  ∫ 10 dx = 10x + C</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Find:  ∫ (6x + 7) dx</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. (3)x² + 7x + C</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. (6)x² + 7x + C</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. (3)x² + 8x + C</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6 + C</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Integrate term by term: 6x²/2 + 7x + C = (3)x² + 7x + C</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 10 Mathematics Textbook, page 88 — Unit 2: Polynomial Functions 79','Approved','2026-05-25 17:58:56','grade10_math.pdf',88,10,0,0),(98,7,'Practice: Quadratic Equation','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>12x \n−  −  −  −  −  − \n+  +  +  +  +  +  \n+  +  + \nx − 2 \n−  −  −  −  −  − \n−  −  −  −  −  − \n+  +  + \nf \'\'(x) \n+  +  +  +  +  + \n−  −  −  −  −  − \n+  +  + \n0 \n0 \n0 \n0 \n0 \n2</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>From this, by squaring both sides, you get (x − h)2 + (y − k)2 = r2</code></li><li><code>If the centre of a circle is at the origin (i.e. h = 0, k = 0), then the above equation</code></li><li><code>= The above equation is called the standard form of equation of a circle, with centre</code></li><li><code>h = k = 0 and r = 8</code></li><li><code>Therefore, the equation of the circle is (x − 0)2 + (y − 0)2 = 82.</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Example 2  Write down the standard form of the equation of a circle with the given centre and radius. a C (0, 0), r = 8 b C (2, –7), r = 9 Solution a h = k = 0 and r = 8 Therefore, the equation of the circle is (x − 0)2 + (y − 0)2 = 82.</li><li>Example 3  Write the standard form of the equation of the circle with centre at C (2, 3) and that passes through the point P (7, –3). x y P(x, y) r C(h, k) •</li><li>116 Example 11 Find the equations of the lines tangent to the graph of   f (x) = x3 + 1 at a x = −2 b x = −1 c x = 2 Solution      f \'(a) = ( ) ( )</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 13  and  x - y = -7</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 3, y = 10</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 10, y = 3</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 4, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 13, y = -7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 6  →  x = 3;  then y = 13 - 3 = 10</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  4x = 16</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 3</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Divide both sides by 4: x = 16 / 4 = 4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  8x = 96</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 12</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 104</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 11</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 13</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — Divide both sides by 8: x = 96 / 8 = 12</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 15 = 16</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 3</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. -2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Subtract 15 from both sides: x = 16 - 15 = 1</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 190','Approved','2026-05-26 11:25:18','grade12_math.pdf',190,11,0,0),(99,2,'Practice: limit','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 5 Statistics and Probability \n151 \nDefinition 5.5 \nThe first and the last elements of a given class interval are called class limits. Example 4 For the above table, give the lower and upper class limits for the second \nand the fourth classes. Solution: \nFor the second class 6 – 11, 6 is called the lower class limit and 11 is \ncalled the upper class limit, while the lower limit and the upper limit of \nthe fourth class are 18 and 23 respectively.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>of a function f at a point x = a as x approaches a from the right, lim</code></li><li><code>x approaches a from the right, then L is said to be the right hand limit of f at x = a.</code></li><li><code>x approaches b from the left. Then L is said to be the left hand limit of f at x = b.</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Example 4 For the above table, give the lower and upper class limits for the second and the fourth classes. Solution: For the second class 6 – 11, 6 is called the lower class limit and 11 is called the upper class limit, while the lower limit and the upper limit of the fourth class are 18 and 23 respectively. Exercise 5.1 Exercise 5.1</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Evaluate:  lim(x → 2)  (x² - 4) / (x - 2)</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 3</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 5</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Factor numerator: (x-2)(x+2)/(x-2) = x+2  →  at x=2: 2+2 = 4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Evaluate:  lim(x → 2)  (x + 7)</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Direct substitution: 2 + 7 = 9</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Evaluate:  lim(x → 7)  (x² - 49) / (x - 7)</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 49</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 15</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 13</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Factor numerator: (x-7)(x+7)/(x-7) = x+7  →  at x=7: 7+7 = 14</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Evaluate:  lim(x → ∞)  (3x + 6) / (5x + 8)</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 4/5</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 3/5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/2</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 3/4</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide numerator and denominator by x: limit = 3/5 = 3/5</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 159','Approved','2026-05-26 11:33:34','grade11_math.pdf',159,11,0,0),(100,7,'Practice: Quadratic Equation','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>A system of linear equation in two variables can be solved by one of the \nfollowing methods: graphically, or by substitution or by elimination. The absolute value of a number 𝑥, denoted by |𝑥|, is defined as the distance \n|𝑥| from zero on a number line. For any non- negative number 𝑝, |𝑥| = 𝑝  means 𝑥= 𝑝  or 𝑥= −𝑝.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>9. For any non- negative number 𝑝, |𝑥| = 𝑝  means 𝑥= 𝑝  or 𝑥= −𝑝.</code></li><li><code>10. An equation of the form 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0  where a, b, c, ∈ R and 𝑎≠0  is a</code></li><li><code>can be used to solve a quadratic equation 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0</code></li><li><code>12. If the quadratic equation, 𝑎𝑥2 + 𝑏𝑥+ 𝑐= 0, has roots</code></li><li><code>13. The discriminant of quadratic equations, 𝐷= 𝑏2 −4𝑎𝑐,</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>function is not sufficient. For example, the motion of an object thrown either upward or downward is modeled by a quadratic function. The graph of a quadratic function is a curve called a parabola. Parabolas may open upward or downward and vary in” width” or” steepness”, but they all have the same basic” U” shape. All parabolas are symmetric with respect to a line called the axis of symmetry. A parabola intersects its axis of symmetry at a point called the vertex of the parabola. Many quadratic </li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Solve the system:  x + y = 13  and  x - y = -7</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. x = 10, y = 3</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. x = 4, y = 9</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. x = 3, y = 10</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. x = 13, y = -7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Add equations: 2x = 6  →  x = 3;  then y = 13 - 3 = 10</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Solve for x:  10x = 80</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 70</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 7</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 9</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Divide both sides by 10: x = 80 / 10 = 8</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Solve for x:  x + 19 = 23</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 4</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Subtract 19 from both sides: x = 23 - 19 = 4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Solve for x:  10x = 10</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 0</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 1</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 2</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 11</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Divide both sides by 10: x = 10 / 10 = 1</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 9 Mathematics Textbook, page 159 — Unit 1: Relations and Functions 41','Approved','2026-05-26 11:42:01','grade9_math.pdf',159,9,0,0),(101,3,'Practice: integration','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 1 Sequences and Series \n31 Solution \nThe nth rectangle has unit length \nand height 2−n units. Hence, the \narea of the nth rectangle is An = 2−n \nsquare unit. =\nr\nr\nr\nr  \n6  \nSuppose a ball is dropped from a height of h m and always rebounds to r of the \nheight from which it falls.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>area of the nth rectangle is An = 2−n</code></li><li><code>square units = 1 unit square.</code></li><li><code>From this, by squaring both sides, you get (x − h)2 + (y − k)2 = r2</code></li><li><code>If the centre of a circle is at the origin (i.e. h = 0, k = 0), then the above equation</code></li><li><code>= The above equation is called the standard form of equation of a circle, with centre</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Example 2  Write down the standard form of the equation of a circle with the given centre and radius. a C (0, 0), r = 8 b C (2, –7), r = 9 Solution a h = k = 0 and r = 8 Therefore, the equation of the circle is (x − 0)2 + (y − 0)2 = 82.</li><li>Example 3  Write the standard form of the equation of the circle with centre at C (2, 3) and that passes through the point P (7, –3). x y P(x, y) r C(h, k) •</li><li>Example 18 Discuss the continuity of the function 2 ( ) 3 16 f x x =</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> Find:  ∫ 2 dx</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 39</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2x²/2 + C</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 3x + C</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 2x + C</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 1x + C</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — ∫ constant dx = constant·x + C  →  ∫ 2 dx = 2x + C</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> Evaluate:  ∫₀^6 x dx</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 39</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 18</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 36</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 19</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — ∫ x dx = x²/2  →  [6²/2 - 0²/2] = 36/2 = 18</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> Find:  ∫ 4x^2 dx</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 39</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. (4)x^3 + C</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. (4/3)x^2 + C</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. (8)x^1 + C</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. (4/3)x^3 + C</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — Power rule: ∫ ax^n dx = a·x^(n+1)/(n+1) + C = 4/3x^3 + C</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> Evaluate:  ∫₀^7 x dx</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 39</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 49</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 25</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 49/2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — ∫ x dx = x²/2  →  [7²/2 - 0²/2] = 49/2 = 49/2</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 39','Approved','2026-05-26 13:00:01','grade12_math.pdf',39,11,0,0),(102,1,'Practice: set','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 7 The Set of Complex Numbers \n283 \n7.5  ARGAND DIAGRAM AND POLAR \nREPRESENTATION OF COMPLEX NUMBERS \nThis sub-unit begins by considering the Cartesian coordinate axes. Previously, you have \nused a pair of numbers to represent a point in a plane. The main task of this section is to \nset up a one-to-one correspondence between the set of points in a plane and the set of \ncomplex numbers.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>When is (a, b) = (c, d)? c</code></li><li><code>Let S be the set of the first 10 prime numbers. Define a function f : S → ℕ by f (n) = n2− 2.</code></li><li><code>For example f (1) = 1, f (2) = 1 + 1 = 2, f (3) = 1 + 2 + 1 = 4.</code></li><li><code>Let Q(x, y) be the open proposition \"x + y = x – y\".  If the universal set for x and y</code></li><li><code>(∀n) (∃m) (n + m = 0)</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>For example f (1) = 1, f (2) = 1 + 1 = 2, f (3) = 1 + 2 + 1 = 4. a    Evaluate f (10), f (15) and f (n) .</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> A survey of 21 students found 6 study Math and 14 study Science. 2 study both, and 3 study neither. How many study Math ONLY?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 4</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 15</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — Math only = n(Math) - n(Math∩Science) = 6 - 2 = 4</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"1\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> In a class, 20 students play football and 17 play basketball. 1 play both sports. How many play at least one sport?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 16</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 36</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 19</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 37</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: B</strong> — n(A∪B) = 20 + 17 - 1 = 36</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> A survey of 32 students found 26 study Math and 15 study Science. 12 study both, and 2 study neither. How many study Math ONLY?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 26</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 12</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 14</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Math only = n(Math) - n(Math∩Science) = 26 - 12 = 14</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> A survey of 46 students found 30 study Math and 14 study Science. 5 study both, and 8 study neither. How many study Math ONLY?</p><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Curriculum Source</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 16</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 5</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 25</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 14</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — Math only = n(Math) - n(Math∩Science) = 30 - 5 = 25</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 291','Approved','2026-05-26 13:02:24','grade11_math.pdf',291,11,0,0),(103,5,'Practice: probablity','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 1 Sequences and Series \n31 Solution \nThe nth rectangle has unit length \nand height 2−n units. Hence, the \narea of the nth rectangle is An = 2−n \nsquare unit. =\nr\nr\nr\nr  \n6  \nSuppose a ball is dropped from a height of h m and always rebounds to r of the \nheight from which it falls.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>area of the nth rectangle is An = 2−n</code></li><li><code>square units = 1 unit square.</code></li><li><code>From this, by squaring both sides, you get (x − h)2 + (y − k)2 = r2</code></li><li><code>If the centre of a circle is at the origin (i.e. h = 0, k = 0), then the above equation</code></li><li><code>= The above equation is called the standard form of equation of a circle, with centre</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Example 2  Write down the standard form of the equation of a circle with the given centre and radius. a C (0, 0), r = 8 b C (2, –7), r = 9 Solution a h = k = 0 and r = 8 Therefore, the equation of the circle is (x − 0)2 + (y − 0)2 = 82.</li><li>Example 3  Write the standard form of the equation of the circle with centre at C (2, 3) and that passes through the point P (7, –3). x y P(x, y) r C(h, k) •</li><li>Example 18 Discuss the continuity of the function 2 ( ) 3 16 f x x =</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-questions\"><h3>Practice Questions</h3><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"3\"><p><strong>Q1.</strong> A bag contains 7 identical objects, of which 5 are white ball. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a white ball?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 60</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2/7</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 5/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 7/5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 5/7</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: D</strong> — P(white ball) = favorable / total = 5/7</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"0\"><p><strong>Q2.</strong> A fair 6-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 5?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 60</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 1/6</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 5/6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 1/5</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 1/6</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: A</strong> — There is 1 favorable outcome (rolling a 5) out of 6 total. P(5) = 1/6</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q3.</strong> A bag contains 10 identical objects, of which 8 are king. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a king?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag extreme\">Exam-style from Extreme Mathematics</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 60</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 2/10</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 8/2</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 8/10</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 10/8</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(king) = favorable / total = 8/10</div></div><div class=\"rag-question\" data-correct=\"2\"><p><strong>Q4.</strong> A bag contains 8 identical objects, of which 2 are jack. If one object is drawn at random, what is the probability it is a jack?</p><span class=\"rag-style-tag grade\">Foundational — Grade Textbook</span><span class=\"rag-src-tag\">Source: Ethiopian Grade 11 Mathematics Textbook, page 60</span><ul class=\"rag-options\"><li data-idx=\"0\" class=\"rag-option\">A. 6/8</li><li data-idx=\"1\" class=\"rag-option\">B. 2/6</li><li data-idx=\"2\" class=\"rag-option\">C. 2/8</li><li data-idx=\"3\" class=\"rag-option\">D. 8/2</li></ul><div class=\"rag-answer\" style=\"display:none\"><strong>Answer: C</strong> — P(jack) = favorable / total = 2/8</div></div></div>','Ethiopian Grade 12 Mathematics Textbook, page 39','Approved','2026-05-26 13:03:11','grade12_math.pdf',39,11,0,0),(104,5,'Practice: probablity','<div class=\"rag-explanation\"><h3>Curriculum Overview</h3><p>Unit 1 Sequences and Series \n31 Solution \nThe nth rectangle has unit length \nand height 2−n units. Hence, the \narea of the nth rectangle is An = 2−n \nsquare unit. =\nr\nr\nr\nr  \n6  \nSuppose a ball is dropped from a height of h m and always rebounds to r of the \nheight from which it falls.</p></div>\n<div class=\"rag-formulas\"><h3>Key Formulas</h3><ul><li><code>area of the nth rectangle is An = 2−n</code></li><li><code>square units = 1 unit square.</code></li><li><code>From this, by squaring both sides, you get (x − h)2 + (y − k)2 = r2</code></li><li><code>If the centre of a circle is at the origin (i.e. h = 0, k = 0), then the above equation</code></li><li><code>= The above equation is called the standard form of equation of a circle, with centre</code></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"rag-examples\"><h3>Worked Examples from Textbook</h3><ul><li>Example 2  Write down the standard form of the equation of a circle with the given centre and radius. a C (0, 0), r = 8 b C (2, –7), r = 9 Solution a h = k = 0 and r = 8 Th